WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.030
SPEAKER 1: I first met our next
speaker and his lovely wife,

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.540
Penny, way back in the last
decade of the last century.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.750
If that sounds like a
long time ago, it is.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.100
It was.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.835
At the time, I was studying
for my PhD in political science

00:00:13.835 --> 00:00:15.210
at the Claremont
Graduate School,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.700
and he was the president
of the Claremont Institute.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.192
And what I quickly learned
about him is something

00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:21.900
Hillsdale students
today learn about him,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.520
and that's the fact
that he's a teacher.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.340
He's always teaching.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.110
He didn't teach formal
classes back then,

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:33.580
but he always took the time to
talk to us graduate students.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.840
And by the way, last night I
mentioned his love of tennis.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.170
His love of teaching
extended to the tennis court.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.360
We used to play
with some regularity

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.533
back in those long-ago days.

00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:47.700
He was well-skilled in the
fundamentals of the game,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.980
and he loved to teach
those fundamentals.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.920
Now, what that meant in practice
is that, not only would he

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.200
trounce you on the
court, afterwards he

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.776
would explain exactly
how he trounced you.

00:00:58.776 --> 00:01:00.453
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:00.453 --> 00:01:02.120
I would encourage you
to come and see us

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.519
on the Hillsdale campus,
and what you'll see

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:07.010
is he hasn't changed a bit.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.550
He still loves to teach and now
he's doing it in the classroom.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.170
He offers courses on
a variety of subjects,

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:16.490
including Aristotle, the
Constitution, CS Lewis,

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.980
and totalitarian novels.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:20.600
And it's been my
privilege to work

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.270
with him at my alma
mater for the past, now,

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:24.463
more than 20 years.

00:01:24.463 --> 00:01:25.880
Please welcome
back to the podium,

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.556
the 12th President of Hillsdale
College, Larry P. Arnn.

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:32.021
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:43.065 --> 00:01:44.940
LARRY P. ARNN: Well, I
thought Mark Steyn was

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.190
going to end up giving the
best speech at this thing,

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.154
but now there's doubt.

00:01:49.154 --> 00:01:52.140
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.400
Thank you all for being here.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.380
I'm going to excuse myself
after lunch because I'm

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.710
having lunch with the governor.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.710
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:06.906 --> 00:02:10.300
And that's happening because
I'm a really important guy.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.350
That's happening because he's
wanting to save the country,

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:21.880
and he wants charter schools,
and we know a lot about that.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.788
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:30.670
And I've had a new
thought in the last month

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.830
that I'll tell you about.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:36.220
For some reason, we
have education centers

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.330
popping up all over the place,
and we didn't really plan them.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.010
Well, we did one in Washington,
DC, on Capitol Hill.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:47.090
We got a graduate school there
and a lot going on there.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:50.590
But there's one in
Somers, Connecticut now.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:52.090
That's on the
Massachusetts border.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:53.860
Have you heard about that?

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:56.920
It was given to us by
the founder and builder

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.870
of Friendly Ice Cream.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.980
And it's a beautiful property,
and it has an exact replica

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.350
of Monticello on it.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:06.815
And I thought, OK.

00:03:06.815 --> 00:03:08.358
And he called up
and said, I'm going

00:03:08.358 --> 00:03:10.900
to give you this property, and
I want to give you $25 million

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.243
in endowment to take care of it.

00:03:14.243 --> 00:03:16.558
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:18.873 --> 00:03:20.870
And I looked around
for a minute--

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.500
couldn't think of
a reason to say no.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:29.080
Now there's something
like that in California--

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.520
maybe two things.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.250
And then the other
day, I was reading

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.460
about our governor in Michigan.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.200
[CROWD BOOING]

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.290
And I thought, you
know, California,

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.800
Connecticut, Michigan?

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.315
We need to go to a red state.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:50.220
So we may--

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.492
I'm going to talk to
the governor about that.

00:03:52.492 --> 00:03:54.450
And see, it'll be a great
conversation for him,

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.780
because I'm confident--
other governors have

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.610
done this when I bring it up.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.170
Well, we'll help you.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:06.240
Kristi Noem offered to build
us an entire campus in South

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.670
Dakota.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:14.090
And I said, Governor, we
don't take any money from you.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.230
We can't do that.

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:17.540
She said, well, maybe
I could raise it.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:21.170
Maybe, I said, but
that would be a lot.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.810
So I don't want anything
from the governor

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:28.880
except to talk to him about
it and ask him to anticipate

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.690
what's going to happen.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.740
And I'll tell you what we're
going to do in these education

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.780
centers.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:39.890
We now have accumulated
registered online students,

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:43.460
about three and 1/2 million.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.330
We have 26 charter schools
and eight more opening

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:50.351
in the next 18 months.

00:04:50.351 --> 00:04:52.836
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:58.990
We've got curricula
and we've got schools

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:01.450
that we advise in
the running of.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:04.060
And we have hundreds
of teachers come

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:08.650
to our campus in the
summer, and we're getting

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:10.520
to know a lot about that.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:14.750
And so we're going to open
a homeschool division.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.004
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.480
We're going to do online
courses for credit.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.490
It means you'll
actually have to study.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:33.397
And first,
dual-enrollment courses.

00:05:33.397 --> 00:05:34.980
We're going to start
a master's degree

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.140
program in classical
education pretty soon,

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.230
I think, so that we
can staff these schools

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:45.240
because the great bottleneck
is headmasters, and after that,

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:46.470
teachers.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:51.000
And teaching is a
very high calling.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.380
And they're not trained that
way anymore, and that's too bad.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:59.460
So these education centers will
be where those people can come

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:02.880
somewhere local
and do in-person--

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.200
like this, sort of.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:10.490
And so it's a grand
adventure, and that's

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.440
why I won't be here
at lunch today.

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:20.110
Now, this crisis that we're in--
that's a good segue into that,

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.620
right, because this is the
most serious kind of thing,

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:25.210
in my opinion.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:30.770
How do we understand it?

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:31.700
What is it?

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.860
And then what do we do about it?

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:38.960
And I'll mention some examples
from the past that give hope.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.150
Think of the resources
that are gathered

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:45.000
by the ruling class, because
that's what they are now.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:53.570
The institutions
in which we learn,

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.930
starting with the
most elite ones, where

00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:02.420
the fancypants
people are trained,

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:08.300
they are almost
entirely woke now.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:10.310
Williams College-- I
have a friend there,

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:11.780
teaches in political science.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:15.620
And he wrote an article in Aero
Magazine, "The Woke Movement

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:16.550
at Williams College."

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:17.342
You can look it up.

00:07:17.342 --> 00:07:20.870
Aero is A-E-R-O.
It's free online.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.870
It's a terrifying thing.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:28.790
Basically, school has stopped
happening because everybody's

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:30.020
afraid to say anything.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:40.800
But that's just at the top
because the staple university

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.320
in America today
is of two kinds.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:47.520
The modal type, the one that's
most common, it's either

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:51.570
a second or third-tier
state university, where

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.420
almost everybody
goes to college now,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:57.630
or else it's a broke
liberal-arts college.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:02.670
And I think Forbes says
there are 963 of those

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:06.420
and that maybe 30% of
them are in some kind

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.200
of imminent trouble.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:12.170
And what happens at
those state universities

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:16.460
is that they're clones
of the most elite

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:20.150
to the best of their ability--
and very common, that is.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:24.170
It's the third-tier ones,
and second-tier ones some,

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:29.720
that train the teachers,
and they are teaching

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.600
them all the rightest stuff.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.520
And they're heavily
under the influence

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:36.440
of the state
education bureaucracy

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:38.390
in every state which
is, by the way,

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:40.460
more than half the
budget in every state.

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:47.590
And I'll mention about that,
that in the public schools

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:48.760
in America--

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:52.630
this is a shocking fact,
and nobody talks about it.

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:54.700
Did you know that more
than half the employees

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.830
in public education
today are not teachers?

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:02.900
Do you know what the number
is in a charter school?

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:08.230
It's about six teachers for
every non-teaching employee.

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:11.560
So first of all, more
than half the budget

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:14.290
never gets anywhere
near the school.

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:16.870
And what do those people do?

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:23.050
Well, they design
what's to be taught.

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:25.300
You know, like that needs
to be done, by the way.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:29.860
I mean, if the one in this room
who knows the least about it

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:34.780
set out honestly to
construct a basic curriculum

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:37.720
along the
long-established lines,

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:41.390
you would do it more or less
successfully in about a month.

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:43.510
It's not that hard, right?

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:46.090
Human beings grow up at about
the same rate they always did.

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:48.190
They need to know
the same things.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:50.360
They need to be good at
the human skills-- reading,

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.030
writing, and arithmetic.

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:55.900
They need natural
history, natural science,

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:58.300
and the story of human
beings, and literature

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:00.400
and philosophy and history.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.590
And then you've done it, right?

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:04.090
That's everything.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:04.720
But no.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:08.720
Constantly inventing new
ways to teach them to read,

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.090
for example.

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:13.240
Whereas in our charter schools,
in our Hillsdale Academy,

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:15.050
we've never graduated
a kindergartener

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:17.230
who couldn't read.

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:20.530
And the reason is they
can already read--

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:21.790
I mean, in principle.

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:29.380
Because my granddaughter,
that makes me pity you,

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:33.280
she's already teaching
herself to talk.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:36.520
And she's going to
be like her mother.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:38.177
She's never going to shut up.

00:10:38.177 --> 00:10:39.670
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:40.990
But then once you talk--

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:42.970
because what happens
when you talk?

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:47.830
You say, glasses.

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:50.030
There are many pairs
of glasses, right?

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:53.500
And we don't look at
cue cards or flash cards

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:57.100
to learn what that is or to see
a whole variety of them, right?

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:00.280
We just look at it, and we
can see the essence of it.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.850
And then that word,
glasses, that's

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.820
the equivalent of the
mark that's on a page--

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:08.500
G-L-A-S-S-E-S.

00:11:09.898 --> 00:11:11.440
And so all you got
to do-- the reason

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.450
it works is because
they've already

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:15.730
taught themselves the sounds.

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:17.830
That's why phonics works, right?

00:11:17.830 --> 00:11:21.130
Just apply phonics to
the marks on the page.

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:23.380
And the point is,
a lot of them just

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:26.540
discover that for
themselves, by the way.

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:29.110
But even if they
don't, just teach them

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:31.840
what the sounds are that
go with these letters.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:32.800
And you know what?

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:33.430
I can't do it.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:38.230
But I actually did do
phonics in elementary school,

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:41.748
and that was in the
late-17th century.

00:11:41.748 --> 00:11:44.018
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:51.350
So the point is there's
a huge load in this.

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:54.470
And by the way,
their opinions tend

00:11:54.470 --> 00:12:00.080
to be opinions that would
justify their existence.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:03.320
You see, like a history
teacher or a science teacher

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:06.320
or a lit teacher, they'll
be doing that because they

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:09.500
think that those subjects are
important and they love them.

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.080
Whereas if you're a
regulator of education,

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:16.280
you're bound to think education
needs a lot of regulating.

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.980
And what's wrong, by the
way, with that idea--

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:21.830
I said it this morning
to the first-timers.

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:24.530
If you become a teacher--

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:26.420
I didn't do it until I was 47.

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:30.230
My father was a high
school teacher in Arkansas,

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:33.260
and he was a wonderful man,
and he found his happiness

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:33.920
doing that.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:35.630
Never made any money.

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:38.990
But I started doing it when I
decided to go into the college

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:42.080
business, and my
first job in a college

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:44.120
is the one I've got today.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:45.980
And I started teaching.

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:49.790
And a great truth that
I knew only in principle

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:51.740
was borne in upon me.

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:55.290
And that is, you
don't teach them.

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:57.490
They learn.

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:59.340
You just help them.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:03.510
And if you explain to them the
most beautiful thing you know--

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:06.960
something that might have taken
you years of devotion and love

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:08.940
to learn--

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:10.440
if you don't do it
right way, you'll

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:12.750
just bore the heck out of them.

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:18.560
And the way not to do that is to
ask them before you tell them.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:21.620
It's good to tell them why
this might be important.

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:23.540
But what is that thing?

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:26.030
That's Socrates' question,
that he's always asking--

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:27.558
[GREEK] What is?

00:13:27.558 --> 00:13:28.100
What is this?

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:29.120
What is that?

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:31.740
Right?

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:38.510
Well, education is this
control of a vast engine that

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:41.810
obscures that fact, and
therefore dehumanizes

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:45.380
the kids in a more
general and less intense

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:49.103
way than making them
transgender when

00:13:49.103 --> 00:13:50.270
they're too young to decide.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:58.680
Now, large parts of the way we
gather and report information

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.770
is in concentrated hands--

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:03.900
an element, I think,
of this ruling class.

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.930
I think that what college
professors have done--

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:13.050
because, you know, being
a college professor,

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:17.692
it ain't sexy by today's terms.

00:14:17.692 --> 00:14:18.900
I can tell you what they are.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:21.030
I've interviewed
more than 450 people

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:23.460
for faculty jobs in 21 years.

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:25.050
I just love it.

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:28.230
I've just learned to
respect them so deeply.

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.000
And here's the thing.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.640
They're all used to being among
the smartest people they ever

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:33.403
met.

00:14:33.403 --> 00:14:34.350
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.010
They are, you know?

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:42.810
And they could go to
law school or med school

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:46.800
and there'd be a guaranteed
path to a big income.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:48.050
And they don't.

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:50.280
They get three times
as much education

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:52.560
and they're going to
make a third as much.

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:55.140
Now why do they
make that choice?

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:59.250
The good ones-- and the
ones at Hillsdale are good--

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:00.720
there's a love.

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:01.695
They love.

00:15:01.695 --> 00:15:02.445
They want to know.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:10.420
They too, see, they carry their
excellence inside themselves.

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:14.860
There's a fire that burns in
them to get them all that way.

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:17.040
And then they want to teach.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:23.270
Well, the trouble is,
you'll never get rich,

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:27.290
and you don't really get to
tell anybody adult what to do.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.750
So what if-- and this
is a great engineering

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:32.143
project that was born
in the Progressive Era

00:15:32.143 --> 00:15:33.185
in American universities.

00:15:33.185 --> 00:15:35.720
It came from Germany--

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:40.440
we could be the ones who would
plan the future of society.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.550
Now we will rule.

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:43.790
See, we'll be important.

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:46.400
We'll be powerful.

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:50.740
I once stayed in the suburbs
outside Washington, DC

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:54.280
and went in on the metro.

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:56.900
And I just looked who was there.

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:59.125
And I recognized a
couple of people,

00:15:59.125 --> 00:16:01.000
and I asked a couple of
others who they were.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:04.090
And they were academics going
in to decide what the government

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:05.860
was going to do today.

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:12.160
And that's kind of
intoxicating and strong.

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:14.830
Men and women of affairs, right?

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:21.870
So they dominate journalism now.

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:24.270
And see, journalists are
just like college professors

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:30.270
because what they used to be
was servants of the truth.

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:33.090
That speech that we just heard--

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:35.400
I just invited
Abigail to come teach

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:38.130
because it was disciplined.

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:40.230
And what was it disciplined to?

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:43.260
The facts as she has seen them.

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:45.270
And in the course of it,
she named a whole bunch

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:49.260
of things that were curious to
me, but sounded very plausible.

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:51.570
Like, for example,
there are a lot

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:53.730
of people who have
gender dysphoria.

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:55.930
And I've never doubted
that, and I've never doubted

00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:57.810
they shouldn't have help.

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:00.990
But it's possible
that this could even

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:05.849
be help to some people, and
you shouldn't begrudge that.

00:17:05.849 --> 00:17:09.540
And that means that she
followed what she saw.

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:13.890
And that means she's not
master of the universe.

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:16.890
She's just beholding it.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.930
And this new temper is better.

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:21.030
It's all a contest for power.

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.940
And journalists today,
they go look at things

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:24.869
and they report them.

00:17:24.869 --> 00:17:27.030
That's what they're
supposed to do.

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:29.280
And they have to struggle
to do it objectively

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:30.510
and they got to take care.

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:32.770
It's just like the
scholar's art except it's

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:35.820
stuff that's moving every day.

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:37.980
Well, what if, instead,
they get to decide

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:40.740
who's the next president
of the United States?

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:43.120
That's a cool station.

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:45.900
The Washington Post really
writes these the most.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:47.400
If you read the
stories about who

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:50.400
gets to go to the White
House press conference,

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:52.140
you can tell the
high journalists

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:54.360
think they own that space.

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:56.790
That's our space, you see?

00:17:56.790 --> 00:17:59.190
And they're the media.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:01.710
They're between
us and something.

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:06.180
And we can't have direct
access to that something.

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:08.880
That's the phenomenon.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:14.040
And I liked it that Trump would
pick nobodies to come in there.

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:16.124
It really ticked them off, see?

00:18:16.124 --> 00:18:17.980
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:22.090
But that space,
that's a space that

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:24.580
belongs to the American
people, and it's

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:26.170
reserved for the
person they elect

00:18:26.170 --> 00:18:27.520
to be the chief executive.

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:31.220
But no, these guys
own that space now.

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:33.195
It's like a bigger deal--

00:18:33.195 --> 00:18:33.695
power.

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:38.785
The major corporations.

00:18:38.785 --> 00:18:41.650
Did you know, this stuff
that went on in the last few

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:44.740
months-- and it's
going on all the time--

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:46.630
threatening this
state, for example,

00:18:46.630 --> 00:18:50.230
right now about a
transgender law.

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:54.760
Well, two years ago, I
think it was, Bezos and Tim

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:58.600
Cook and the whole
gang, they actually

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:02.960
have more money
than everybody else,

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:06.082
if you include
their money in that.

00:19:06.082 --> 00:19:07.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:11.320
And they decided that
they would renounce

00:19:11.320 --> 00:19:14.260
what was called the Friedman
Rule, from Milton Friedman.

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:17.770
And the Friedman Rule was, the
assets of a public corporation

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:19.750
belong to the
shareholders, and they

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:24.350
should be used for the purpose
of enhancing shareholder value.

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:28.240
And if they want to give to
charity, give their own money,

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:31.660
and give the charity money
back to the shareholders.

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:35.350
And they just specifically
renounced that.

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:37.240
And that means what
they were saying,

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:40.150
in the plainest
language, was, we're

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:42.010
going to take other
people's money now,

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:45.100
and we're going to decide
how to affect the society.

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:46.540
Power, you see?

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:48.370
Power.

00:19:48.370 --> 00:19:51.430
I think Steve Jobs is
probably a great man,

00:19:51.430 --> 00:19:53.980
and I think the ones who
follow him are not so great.

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.770
But one of the
reasons was he always

00:19:56.770 --> 00:20:01.990
thought he was conforming to the
demands of beauty and utility

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:03.880
in making his products.

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:07.510
And he was very
disciplined about that.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:12.940
Well, that means that all acts
of creativity of high worth

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:16.120
are also great
acts of obedience.

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:18.820
It's obedience we
don't like anymore.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:20.440
We like to tell
people what to do.

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:22.870
Did you know that obedience
was one of Winston Churchill's

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:26.170
favorite words?

00:20:26.170 --> 00:20:28.540
He always thought
he was obeying.

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:30.834
That's why he was so stubborn.

00:20:30.834 --> 00:20:32.722
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:38.120
Then there's the
regulatory state,

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:41.750
and that's 24 million
people, most of them

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:43.530
at the state level.

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:49.640
But it's very much a system that
influences from the top down.

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:52.610
And they're a class of person.

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:54.770
And they're not what
they used to be.

00:20:54.770 --> 00:20:57.320
Churchill said that
famous thing once--

00:20:57.320 --> 00:21:01.550
no longer civil and
no longer servants.

00:21:01.550 --> 00:21:04.370
Well, there's that going on.

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:07.100
In the early Progressive Era--

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:09.793
RJ Pestritto
publishes about that;

00:21:09.793 --> 00:21:11.960
he's the dean of our graduate
school in the politics

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:13.940
department--

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:18.050
they thought that separation
of powers was no longer needed

00:21:18.050 --> 00:21:20.930
because now there's going to
be a class of person working

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:25.340
in the government who will have
a guaranteed salary that cannot

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:29.150
be reduced, it'll be
enough to support them,

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:32.600
and they'll have tenure
in office forever.

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:36.080
And that means they won't
have any needs or interest,

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:40.680
and so they won't pursue their
needs and interest in politics.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:43.560
If you look at the list of the
10 biggest givers in politics

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:47.370
today, in partisan
elections, all on one side,

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:51.450
the public employee
unions are tops.

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:55.590
Because they're people too,
and they have interests,

00:21:55.590 --> 00:22:00.580
and they like a world in which
people like them decide things

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:03.480
in the name, not of consent--

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:06.105
the popular thing-- in
the name of science.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:13.040
The pandemic is the craziest
thing in the world to me

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:16.310
because we decided
we were going to have

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:20.600
college, up to the point
of threat of arrest,

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:21.350
which I've had.

00:22:24.980 --> 00:22:27.530
But I didn't want to
do that recklessly.

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:30.560
As I tell the students,
if you get sick

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:35.090
and die, although
we can get more,

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:37.910
it'll be very
inconvenient to me.

00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:39.515
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:44.040
I say that all the time.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:46.123
You know, it's a weird
thing at Hillsdale College.

00:22:46.123 --> 00:22:47.784
The mothers like that.

00:22:47.784 --> 00:22:48.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:51.360
They're like Spartan women.

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:53.850
Anyway, I didn't
want them to die.

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:56.480
Disrupt the schedule.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:00.240
And so I tried to find
out, and we found ourselves

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:02.730
in association with
some people that I

00:23:02.730 --> 00:23:04.260
think are just geniuses--

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:08.490
Jay Bhattacharya, Scott
Atlas, Daniel Halperin.

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:16.300
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:21.190
Then after that, it's easy
because I have to obey the law,

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:22.990
if they actually make a law--

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:27.110
although what they make now
is memos from the governor,

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:31.570
and that's not really how
a law gets made, you know.

00:23:31.570 --> 00:23:33.430
And those can be
challenged in court,

00:23:33.430 --> 00:23:35.980
and we are ready to do it.

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:41.200
But did I tell you this?

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:43.300
We have these doctors
from the Mayo Clinic,

00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:48.400
and the Mayo Clinic is very
cautious about the coronavirus

00:23:48.400 --> 00:23:52.710
and they're worried about the
long-term effects and all that.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:54.970
And they always
say, are you worried

00:23:54.970 --> 00:23:56.260
about the long-term effects?

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:59.350
And I said, no, I'm busy.

00:23:59.350 --> 00:24:00.600
I got a lot to do.

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:02.240
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:05.050
But anyway, we can't
know that, right?

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:08.170
It's exactly like the point
made earlier about reversibility

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:09.170
of some of these things.

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:11.320
Well, it hasn't been long
enough to know, right?

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:14.710
Well, anyway, one of them
was kind of critical of me.

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:17.000
Well, he's a
friend, but he said,

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:20.020
you really picked the experts
that were for opening up.

00:24:20.020 --> 00:24:22.280
And I said, sure.

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:24.310
And he said, oh.

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:26.890
He was surprised I'd admit that.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:29.770
And I said, look,
doc, are you guys

00:24:29.770 --> 00:24:32.858
the tops in the world
at the Mayo Clinic?

00:24:32.858 --> 00:24:34.400
And he said, yeah,
we're pretty good.

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:36.620
And I said, how about
these guys at Stanford?

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:39.640
Are they OK?

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:42.970
Expert knowledge--
this ruling class

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:47.170
operates in the
name of expertise.

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:52.000
And that's a different
title to rule than consent,

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:55.210
the consent of equal souls.

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:59.110
And this expertise has got
to be strictly limited.

00:24:59.110 --> 00:25:03.530
As Winston Churchill
explained, expert knowledge

00:25:03.530 --> 00:25:06.280
is narrow knowledge.

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:10.570
Imagine one of those sets
of parents and the kid

00:25:10.570 --> 00:25:14.170
comes under this gender thing.

00:25:14.170 --> 00:25:16.070
What are you going to do?

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:19.030
That's a really hard decision.

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:21.700
Because you're going to
stand up to a lot of people,

00:25:21.700 --> 00:25:24.310
and they're powerful, and
they might take your kid.

00:25:24.310 --> 00:25:27.730
Or you're going to leave the kid
there and try to cope with it.

00:25:27.730 --> 00:25:30.850
There's no good answer
to that question.

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:34.420
But nobody is better
qualified to make the judgment

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:36.118
than a mother.

00:25:36.118 --> 00:25:38.074
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:48.050
So I'm going to give you some
instances where things have

00:25:48.050 --> 00:25:50.120
been like this, where
everything was at stake

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:53.660
and we might lose our freedom.

00:25:53.660 --> 00:25:56.720
And one of them is in 1948--

00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:59.840
1945 to 1951.

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:01.790
After the Second World
War, the socialists

00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:04.880
came in, big majority.

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:08.510
And they're not like these
guys that we've got today.

00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:10.790
They had been announcing
for 50 years what

00:26:10.790 --> 00:26:14.240
they were going to do, and
then they won a big majority.

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:16.370
And Churchill
always said that it

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:19.160
would be the death of
civilization and freedom,

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:22.050
but, by golly, they're
entitled to do it.

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:24.830
And so what we're going
to do is complain about it

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:28.130
and then reverse it later.

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:34.520
And his campaign-- this is a
key thing that I want to say

00:26:34.520 --> 00:26:37.000
today--

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:41.050
from 1945 to 1952,
Winston Churchill

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:44.230
led the Conservative Party on
a campaign against socialism

00:26:44.230 --> 00:26:49.590
under the title "Socialism
Versus The People."

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:50.340
You see?

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:54.630
And he would point out to them
that practical decisions always

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:57.360
involve a hundred factors.

00:26:57.360 --> 00:27:02.570
And by the way, we make them
every day, every one of us.

00:27:02.570 --> 00:27:06.240
There's no more intense or
practical or consequential

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:10.250
decision than how
to raise your kid.

00:27:10.250 --> 00:27:14.630
Well, Mr. Douglas Jay
of the Home Office

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:17.390
in the Attlee socialist
administration

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:18.950
said a famous thing.

00:27:18.950 --> 00:27:20.210
And it echoes now.

00:27:20.210 --> 00:27:22.850
If you know anything about
modern British history,

00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:24.470
you'll know this.

00:27:24.470 --> 00:27:28.490
He said, mothers do
not always understand

00:27:28.490 --> 00:27:30.860
how to feed their children.

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:34.140
The gentlemen in Whitehall--
that's the governing center

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:34.880
of Britain--

00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:39.410
the gentlemen in
Whitehall know better.

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:41.690
And he made the terrible
mistake of saying

00:27:41.690 --> 00:27:43.724
that in the hearing
of Winston Churchill.

00:27:43.724 --> 00:27:45.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:47.960
And Churchill rang
the changes on that.

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:50.750
But you see what that means?

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:55.610
I just named a
four-part, vast apparatus

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:58.852
that is working together
and that controls, directly

00:27:58.852 --> 00:28:01.310
and indirectly, more than half
of the economy in the United

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:04.520
States and Europe.

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:07.730
That is a formidable force.

00:28:07.730 --> 00:28:09.500
It's huge.

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:12.050
And how are you
going to stop it?

00:28:12.050 --> 00:28:15.380
And it's proceeding
under the principle

00:28:15.380 --> 00:28:22.400
that you can't teach a kid
to read without expertise,

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:24.872
which is stupid.

00:28:24.872 --> 00:28:26.330
Because first of
all, a lot of them

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:28.710
learn to read on their own.

00:28:28.710 --> 00:28:32.210
And so that's the point.

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:34.340
That's, by the way,
why it will eventually

00:28:34.340 --> 00:28:39.200
become ugly and despotic,
despite kind intentions.

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:44.050
Because this is the thing
that has to justify itself,

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:48.110
and it's furiously at
work on that all the time.

00:28:52.260 --> 00:29:01.710
This happened in 1776, in 1860,
in 1945 in Britain, and in 1979

00:29:01.710 --> 00:29:04.760
in Britain.

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:07.760
Things were as bad as this
in all those occasions.

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:10.340
Now, I'm not going to
talk a lot about Russia

00:29:10.340 --> 00:29:14.930
in 1917 because that's
a counterexample.

00:29:14.930 --> 00:29:18.770
In other words, the Russian
Revolution succeeded,

00:29:18.770 --> 00:29:24.053
and it succeeded by
heartbreakingly narrow margins.

00:29:24.053 --> 00:29:25.970
And the lives of a
hundred-million people were

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:29.610
at stake, and they lost
their lives because of that--

00:29:29.610 --> 00:29:30.950
well, at world communism.

00:29:30.950 --> 00:29:34.100
Russia was not as good
at killing as China.

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:38.420
It was maybe 35
million in Russia.

00:29:38.420 --> 00:29:42.110
And so that's a sad one, right?

00:29:42.110 --> 00:29:46.310
But those won the American
Revolution, the Civil War,

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:49.790
the rise of Margaret
Thatcher in Britain,

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:53.900
and the post-war Churchill--
only time he became prime

00:29:53.900 --> 00:29:55.970
minister, in 1951--

00:29:55.970 --> 00:29:58.170
and his combat
against socialism.

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:03.140
Which, by the way, that
1951 to '55 administration

00:30:03.140 --> 00:30:06.080
got rid of part of socialism,
and Margaret Thatcher

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:08.575
got rid of the rest.

00:30:08.575 --> 00:30:10.700
And I'll tell you what
those things have in common.

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:18.703
First of all, a
movement started.

00:30:18.703 --> 00:30:20.370
Notice how many people
are in this room?

00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:28.140
Last night, people would
say, but the American people

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:30.510
accept this now.

00:30:30.510 --> 00:30:33.270
Why is there no
movement against it?

00:30:33.270 --> 00:30:36.180
And to both of them
I said, look around.

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:39.090
I mean, I'm telling you.

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:44.340
We at the college, whatever
line of operation we're in--

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:50.430
admissions, faculty hiring,
events, charter schools,

00:30:50.430 --> 00:30:54.000
online students-- we can't
beat them off with a stick.

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:58.140
And it makes a person
optimistic when he sees that.

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:05.370
Because we have this big
national mess that we've made,

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:08.370
but it all radiates from
a tight, little world

00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:11.160
that we're all in,
and we get to measure

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:16.050
the effects of what we do and
say before our eyes every day.

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:20.700
It's just so fine and such a
grand thing to be able to do.

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:23.490
And it helps a lot.

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:25.500
It makes it better, we think.

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:28.380
So there's a movement.

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:36.210
And the next part is greatness--

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:40.170
some great leader.

00:31:40.170 --> 00:31:44.640
And that's extremely
unpredictable.

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:48.120
In the classic
works, statesmanship,

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:49.500
which is a form of art--

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:53.760
a very difficult form of
human making, or art--

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:57.500
is understood as a
synonym for chance.

00:31:57.500 --> 00:31:59.610
In Churchill's
writing, very often,

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:03.840
because chance is the
intersection of two causes

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:06.180
that produce a third
result that neither of them

00:32:06.180 --> 00:32:09.270
would have produced on its own.

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:11.520
And two independent
causes, right?

00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:17.680
So if snow gets on the
branch, the branch breaks off.

00:32:17.680 --> 00:32:19.160
That's nature.

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:21.070
Saw off the branch.

00:32:21.070 --> 00:32:22.610
That's art.

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:25.250
Saw off the branch on the
top of the head of somebody

00:32:25.250 --> 00:32:26.760
standing below.

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:29.690
That's either chance
or a misdemeanor.

00:32:29.690 --> 00:32:32.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:37.970
And so, why would you think
that these extremely rare

00:32:37.970 --> 00:32:42.570
and artful people
would also be chance?

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:46.880
Well, the reason is, can't
control when they show up

00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:49.690
and sometimes you
really need them.

00:32:49.690 --> 00:32:52.490
Now would be good.

00:32:52.490 --> 00:32:57.700
But they didn't
make the movements.

00:32:57.700 --> 00:33:00.700
Those started because
people saw something

00:33:00.700 --> 00:33:02.260
and it disturbed them.

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:06.580
Nobody was worried about Great
Britain before 1763 in America.

00:33:06.580 --> 00:33:10.390
But then they won the
big war with France,

00:33:10.390 --> 00:33:12.220
and they've got this
whacking big empire,

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:15.040
and it just comes to them,
maybe we ought to use it.

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:16.600
And then just so
happens over there,

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:18.550
they had a terrible
failure of statesmanship.

00:33:18.550 --> 00:33:21.760
Because the great men in
Britain at that time--

00:33:21.760 --> 00:33:23.740
Edmund Burke and William Pitt--

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:25.900
were friends of the
American Revolution.

00:33:25.900 --> 00:33:28.690
No, they were governed by
Lord North, who was an idiot.

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:34.750
But they started
doing things to people

00:33:34.750 --> 00:33:38.790
who'd been governing
themselves for 150 years,

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:40.900
and they didn't like it.

00:33:40.900 --> 00:33:46.850
And then there was a
kind of an ignitor.

00:33:46.850 --> 00:33:49.370
Tom Paine wrote Common Sense.

00:33:49.370 --> 00:33:53.190
Before that, George Whitfield,
who's the greatest preacher,

00:33:53.190 --> 00:33:53.690
probably.

00:33:53.690 --> 00:33:57.620
He was the Billy Graham
of the 18th century,

00:33:57.620 --> 00:34:02.180
and he would get 30,000 people
to come and hear him preach.

00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:06.170
And he was preaching about
faith, but also freedom.

00:34:06.170 --> 00:34:10.170
And he became big friends
with Ben Franklin,

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:12.050
who was not
particularly orthodox

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:13.604
in his Christian views.

00:34:13.604 --> 00:34:14.760
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:17.100
And they became friends,
because Ben Franklin went.

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:21.980
And he said, no way he
can talk to 30,000 people.

00:34:21.980 --> 00:34:24.650
And so he went and
stood by the stage.

00:34:24.650 --> 00:34:28.070
And he walked away
and counted his paces

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:31.715
until he could just barely
hear him, but could hear him.

00:34:31.715 --> 00:34:33.590
And then he measured--
you know, Ben Franklin

00:34:33.590 --> 00:34:35.570
was kind of a lunatic.

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:39.949
I mean, a very great man,
but he was very meticulous.

00:34:39.949 --> 00:34:43.040
He measured the space.

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:45.199
And how many people
could stand in the space?

00:34:45.199 --> 00:34:47.600
30,000.

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:52.159
Now, those things were sort
of catalysts, or ignitors.

00:34:52.159 --> 00:34:54.560
But then somebody came
along to guide all of that.

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:58.220
And you know, don't think that
the situation is more difficult

00:34:58.220 --> 00:35:01.290
now than it was in 1776.

00:35:01.290 --> 00:35:03.890
A people who had never
had an army, never

00:35:03.890 --> 00:35:06.020
had a government
of their own, were

00:35:06.020 --> 00:35:08.240
about to make war on the
greatest power on Earth.

00:35:13.510 --> 00:35:19.550
People say to me
sometimes, we could never

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:26.840
have a great revival like
we had in 1858 to 1860

00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:31.140
because people don't pay
attention to politics.

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:34.980
30,000 people came to listen
to the Lincoln-Douglas debates,

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:37.110
and I urge you to read them.

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:38.400
They're sublime.

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:42.810
Douglas is very artful
and very powerful.

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:47.630
Lincoln is sublime, and
he destroyed Douglas.

00:35:47.630 --> 00:35:50.360
And yeah, 30,000 people--
they went on three and 1/2,

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:51.200
four hours--

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:51.860
each one.

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:53.330
There were eight of them.

00:35:53.330 --> 00:35:56.780
And then they came on their
horses and they sat in the sun.

00:35:56.780 --> 00:35:59.460
And they say, people
haven't done that since.

00:35:59.460 --> 00:36:03.410
Well, I said, you know, they
never did that before either,

00:36:03.410 --> 00:36:07.190
but something important
was going on, right?

00:36:07.190 --> 00:36:11.360
This slavery thing was
extremely troubling.

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:14.570
And from every point of view--

00:36:14.570 --> 00:36:17.120
the wicked ones who
thought that they deserved

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:19.533
to own the slaves,
they were afraid they

00:36:19.533 --> 00:36:20.450
were going to lose it.

00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:23.570
But really what they were afraid
of was they couldn't expand it.

00:36:23.570 --> 00:36:28.070
And others, for a mixture of
reasons, the sublime of which

00:36:28.070 --> 00:36:31.010
is equality and
freedom, but also mixed

00:36:31.010 --> 00:36:33.500
around that we don't really
want a lot of Black people

00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:34.670
in this state.

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:39.330
And that's the cauldron that
Abraham Lincoln stood up.

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:40.760
And he was a shabby lawyer.

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:43.610
Everybody thought he was a
hobo when they first saw him.

00:36:43.610 --> 00:36:49.340
And Stephen Douglas
dressed in ruffled shirts

00:36:49.340 --> 00:36:52.940
and had matched horses
on his carriage.

00:36:52.940 --> 00:36:56.570
He just looked like the
aristocracy coming to town.

00:36:56.570 --> 00:37:02.900
And see, people listen
to those arguments.

00:37:02.900 --> 00:37:06.880
That's happening more and more.

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:12.040
In 1948, Churchill
raised that campaign.

00:37:12.040 --> 00:37:16.420
And he was a one-man wrecking
ball in the House of Commons.

00:37:16.420 --> 00:37:18.280
He could just do amazing things.

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:21.460
He, by himself,
built the movement

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:26.200
that got Britain to rearm just
barely enough to survive 1940.

00:37:26.200 --> 00:37:29.558
And at first, he had
five or six backbenchers

00:37:29.558 --> 00:37:31.600
who were in disrepute and
they were the only ones

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:33.280
who listened to him.

00:37:33.280 --> 00:37:34.780
But he was very
good at what he did.

00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:41.110
People kept writing him.

00:37:41.110 --> 00:37:44.250
He started a group
called The Focus.

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:47.850
And the purpose of
it was, we're not

00:37:47.850 --> 00:37:50.880
going to fight with each
other about anything else.

00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:51.780
We're going to unite.

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:54.420
I mean, we can be socialist,
and we can be capitalist,

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:55.800
we can be whatever.

00:37:55.800 --> 00:37:57.180
We're going to
agree that we have

00:37:57.180 --> 00:38:00.270
to stop Adolf Hitler from
conquering Great Britain.

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:01.500
That's all.

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:03.720
Well, there were
hundreds of thousands

00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:05.460
of people in that thing.

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:06.840
And it just grew from nowhere.

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:11.190
It got no help from
the BBC because just

00:38:11.190 --> 00:38:13.320
remember what I said
about learning to read

00:38:13.320 --> 00:38:15.890
and about teaching?

00:38:15.890 --> 00:38:18.410
The salvation of the
United States of America

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:23.330
is in each citizen, and
we're going to find out

00:38:23.330 --> 00:38:26.120
if they're still Americans.

00:38:26.120 --> 00:38:29.060
And if they're not,
it's already over.

00:38:29.060 --> 00:38:31.850
But I think they are.

00:38:31.850 --> 00:38:35.450
And they are going to
produce the conditions

00:38:35.450 --> 00:38:41.470
for a turnaround, or else those
conditions won't be produced.

00:38:41.470 --> 00:38:44.570
Margaret Thatcher-- I happened
to be living there at the time.

00:38:44.570 --> 00:38:47.700
I went over there
to get my girl.

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:52.330
By the way, I was very
relieved that it's

00:38:52.330 --> 00:38:56.115
rare for women in their 50s
and 60s to go transgender.

00:38:56.115 --> 00:38:58.055
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:09.526
I told Penny that, and she said,
we should ask her about men.

00:39:09.526 --> 00:39:11.398
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:18.430
I was working on the official
biography of Winston Churchill.

00:39:18.430 --> 00:39:20.410
And you know, that was great.

00:39:20.410 --> 00:39:23.830
But then Margaret
Thatcher became the leader

00:39:23.830 --> 00:39:27.460
of the Conservative Party, and
then prime minister in 1979,

00:39:27.460 --> 00:39:30.580
right in the middle of the time
that I was living there with my

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:35.680
soon to be Mrs. And it was
just the most thrilling thing

00:39:35.680 --> 00:39:37.120
you ever saw.

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:38.350
I like tennis.

00:39:38.350 --> 00:39:40.960
And so we were there, and
we'd watch Wimbledon on TV.

00:39:40.960 --> 00:39:45.760
That was the McEnroe
versus Borg years.

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:48.940
And this was better.

00:39:48.940 --> 00:39:52.000
Margaret Thatcher arguing
with James Callaghan,

00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:54.220
the socialist prime minister.

00:39:54.220 --> 00:40:00.490
And the adjective they
used for her was shrill.

00:40:00.490 --> 00:40:02.500
She's shrill, right?

00:40:02.500 --> 00:40:03.710
Well, she's a woman.

00:40:03.710 --> 00:40:05.470
She didn't sound like a man.

00:40:05.470 --> 00:40:07.180
But Lord, what she
wasn't was shrill.

00:40:07.180 --> 00:40:09.640
She was tough.

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:13.750
And then she broke him down.

00:40:13.750 --> 00:40:17.320
And he, by the way, was
the last great taunter

00:40:17.320 --> 00:40:21.040
of Winston Churchill,
early in Callaghan's career

00:40:21.040 --> 00:40:23.390
and late in Churchill's career.

00:40:23.390 --> 00:40:26.580
And so he's pretty
formidable, right?

00:40:26.580 --> 00:40:29.120
And she took him
down and broke him.

00:40:29.120 --> 00:40:31.940
And then what was
their situation?

00:40:31.940 --> 00:40:33.080
It's as bad as this.

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:36.560
First of all, the Labour Party
had a majority and they were

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:38.720
ruled by the Trade
Unions Council,

00:40:38.720 --> 00:40:43.610
a majority of whom were open
members of the Communist Party.

00:40:43.610 --> 00:40:48.110
And then these labor unions
were extremely strong

00:40:48.110 --> 00:40:52.940
and they would shut down
parts of the country.

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:55.370
They'd make a demand, very
often on the government,

00:40:55.370 --> 00:40:57.530
because there were
still some socialized--

00:40:57.530 --> 00:41:01.550
the railroads and the phone
company and some of those--

00:41:01.550 --> 00:41:06.050
and they'd just demand
what they wanted.

00:41:06.050 --> 00:41:10.340
And then in the winter,
they'd stop communication.

00:41:10.340 --> 00:41:12.320
You couldn't get milk.

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:16.150
And they would picket, not only
a company they were in dispute

00:41:16.150 --> 00:41:16.650
with.

00:41:16.650 --> 00:41:20.960
It was picketing private people,
the government, everybody.

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:24.140
But they'd picket their
customers and suppliers too,

00:41:24.140 --> 00:41:27.020
called secondary picketing.

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:30.740
And Edward Heath,
who was a weenie, who

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:34.940
was elected prime minister
before Margaret Thatcher,

00:41:34.940 --> 00:41:37.620
he had promised to stop
this if he got a majority.

00:41:37.620 --> 00:41:39.530
And he got a majority,
and he didn't stop it.

00:41:39.530 --> 00:41:42.710
And he gave in to the unions
in the middle of the winter.

00:41:42.710 --> 00:41:45.190
People were getting cold.

00:41:45.190 --> 00:41:47.660
So she says she
won't do that, right?

00:41:47.660 --> 00:41:48.655
And she gets elected.

00:41:48.655 --> 00:41:49.780
And I can remember the day.

00:41:49.780 --> 00:41:52.660
I was watching the news
because they had shut down

00:41:52.660 --> 00:41:58.600
a part of Yorkshire, up in
the north, the city of York,

00:41:58.600 --> 00:42:00.900
an area of it where
there was an industry

00:42:00.900 --> 00:42:03.310
and a lot of different
companies working together.

00:42:03.310 --> 00:42:05.500
And they just closed
the whole thing down

00:42:05.500 --> 00:42:07.540
and the city wasn't working.

00:42:07.540 --> 00:42:09.850
And the BBC is there reporting.

00:42:09.850 --> 00:42:16.790
And then the reporter heard
something and he looked.

00:42:16.790 --> 00:42:20.280
And he said, oh,
there's a convoy coming.

00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:23.130
And it was buses and trucks.

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:25.980
And what was in the
buses was London cops.

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:29.550
And what was in the trucks
was portable fencing.

00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:33.250
And they arrested
about 5,000 people.

00:42:33.250 --> 00:42:37.110
[LAUGHS] And they put them
in these fences they built.

00:42:37.110 --> 00:42:38.900
And then that was over.

00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:44.340
And then the next day,
the BBC was showing guys

00:42:44.340 --> 00:42:49.680
in dark suits and briefcases
going to union offices

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:51.920
to seize their assets.

00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:53.760
And you know, by 5
o'clock the next day,

00:42:53.760 --> 00:42:57.320
there were negotiations
going on about this.

00:42:57.320 --> 00:43:07.020
And she is the firmest
example of executive action

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:09.780
I've seen with my own eyes.

00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:12.870
Not more form-- I was privileged
to know her pretty well.

00:43:12.870 --> 00:43:15.780
And I was privileged
to know Ronald Reagan.

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:17.940
And I'd always say to
him, Mr. President--

00:43:17.940 --> 00:43:21.480
because I was sometimes
critical of him, to him--

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:23.340
and I'd say, Mr.
President, you're

00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:24.840
the greatest man I ever met.

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:26.340
And he would remember
and say, yeah,

00:43:26.340 --> 00:43:28.080
but you think
Margaret's greater.

00:43:28.080 --> 00:43:29.957
And I'd say-- [SHRUGS]

00:43:29.957 --> 00:43:32.402
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:36.810 --> 00:43:40.040
What is to happen today?

00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:44.780
Well, both options-- just
like in all of these crises

00:43:44.780 --> 00:43:45.710
I've named--

00:43:45.710 --> 00:43:49.550
remember, there was
a movement, and there

00:43:49.550 --> 00:43:55.320
was somebody to guide and
lead it, and it became clear,

00:43:55.320 --> 00:43:56.850
and it was fought through--

00:43:56.850 --> 00:44:01.150
sometimes with
violence, sometimes not.

00:44:01.150 --> 00:44:04.840
Well, once you see that that's
a kind of pattern, that's

00:44:04.840 --> 00:44:07.180
what's happened in the
past, first of all,

00:44:07.180 --> 00:44:09.790
you understand this
is very formidable.

00:44:09.790 --> 00:44:12.640
Because for most of the
American Revolution and most

00:44:12.640 --> 00:44:16.630
of the Civil War, most
of the Second World War--

00:44:16.630 --> 00:44:19.545
a different kind of crisis but,
by the way, similar forces--

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:26.670
the people who
eventually won couldn't

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:29.320
imagine that they could win.

00:44:29.320 --> 00:44:32.840
They thought they were
fighting to the death, you see.

00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:36.300
And that means that we
may have to undergo that.

00:44:36.300 --> 00:44:39.300
But we should take comfort from
things like this, because--

00:44:42.410 --> 00:44:45.830
and this is my final point--

00:44:45.830 --> 00:44:50.350
freedom is a beautiful thing.

00:44:50.350 --> 00:44:54.560
The Constitution of the United
States is a beautiful thing.

00:44:54.560 --> 00:44:56.240
The Declaration
of Independence is

00:44:56.240 --> 00:45:00.950
the most beautiful political
document ever written.

00:45:00.950 --> 00:45:05.570
And in the first line
of Aristotle's Ethics,

00:45:05.570 --> 00:45:07.520
he says that every
choice we make

00:45:07.520 --> 00:45:11.450
and every action we take
and every inquiry we

00:45:11.450 --> 00:45:15.410
take all seem to
aim at some good.

00:45:15.410 --> 00:45:19.130
And the highest form of
the good is the beautiful.

00:45:19.130 --> 00:45:22.020
We all long for that.

00:45:22.020 --> 00:45:23.940
These things that
they're doing now,

00:45:23.940 --> 00:45:25.770
they're dangerous
because they parade

00:45:25.770 --> 00:45:28.020
in the name of the beautiful.

00:45:28.020 --> 00:45:31.140
But if they're not
beautiful, that can't last.

00:45:31.140 --> 00:45:34.290
And that means, just like in
every kid there's the potential

00:45:34.290 --> 00:45:39.000
to read, in every human being
breast there's the potential

00:45:39.000 --> 00:45:41.620
to fight for freedom.

00:45:41.620 --> 00:45:45.880
The thing to do now is to
communicate to everyone

00:45:45.880 --> 00:45:47.880
you know--

00:45:47.880 --> 00:45:51.590
learn, get better, teach.

00:45:51.590 --> 00:45:53.930
Don't make yourself a nuisance.

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:56.546
Well, later that might be right.

00:45:56.546 --> 00:45:57.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:58.820 --> 00:46:02.300
One of the iron rules
in excellent education

00:46:02.300 --> 00:46:04.430
is you can't teach
people who don't

00:46:04.430 --> 00:46:08.190
want to learn because it's
hard for them, you know?

00:46:08.190 --> 00:46:10.940
And that means,
don't annoy people.

00:46:10.940 --> 00:46:11.910
Make it easy.

00:46:11.910 --> 00:46:13.550
If you see something
lovely that you

00:46:13.550 --> 00:46:17.210
want people to read, send
it to some people and say,

00:46:17.210 --> 00:46:20.600
you probably don't have time to
read this, but I found it good.

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:22.510
And then you're not
challenging them.

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:29.180
I get a lot of recommendations
like that, and sometimes

00:46:29.180 --> 00:46:30.680
with a challenge.

00:46:30.680 --> 00:46:32.870
Sometimes people
write, well, if you're

00:46:32.870 --> 00:46:35.810
really serious about your
job, then-- you know?

00:46:35.810 --> 00:46:36.710
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:36.710 --> 00:46:39.779
And I usually write
back, I'm not.

00:46:39.779 --> 00:46:42.640
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:42.640 --> 00:46:45.340
See, just like it's true--

00:46:45.340 --> 00:46:49.010
the thing about a
despotic movement,

00:46:49.010 --> 00:46:52.580
they always know who their
numbers are because everybody's

00:46:52.580 --> 00:46:54.590
sort of bought and paid for.

00:46:54.590 --> 00:46:59.690
Whereas a free movement,
it's of infinite size,

00:46:59.690 --> 00:47:02.210
as many as there are people.

00:47:02.210 --> 00:47:11.660
When Abraham Lincoln called for
75,000 troops in March of 1861,

00:47:11.660 --> 00:47:13.970
he didn't know if they'd come.

00:47:13.970 --> 00:47:16.880
He did know that there was
a hostile army that almost

00:47:16.880 --> 00:47:19.370
had the Capital surrounded.

00:47:19.370 --> 00:47:22.700
And if Maryland seceded-- and it
was about to-- then Washington,

00:47:22.700 --> 00:47:24.800
DC would be encircled.

00:47:24.800 --> 00:47:25.980
He knew that.

00:47:25.980 --> 00:47:30.760
And he didn't know if
there could be any help.

00:47:30.760 --> 00:47:33.420
Well, that's where
we are too, remember.

00:47:33.420 --> 00:47:36.510
Because we're on the free side.

00:47:36.510 --> 00:47:39.570
And that means people who
are free will join us,

00:47:39.570 --> 00:47:42.540
or else they will not.

00:47:42.540 --> 00:47:43.440
Let's get them to.

00:47:43.440 --> 00:47:44.388
Thank you.

00:47:44.388 --> 00:47:46.380
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:13.820 --> 00:48:16.070
Now I want to tell those
of you in the front

00:48:16.070 --> 00:48:18.530
that many of those people
in the back were standing up

00:48:18.530 --> 00:48:19.520
and you were not.

00:48:19.520 --> 00:48:21.984
If you didn't like
my speech, get out.

00:48:21.984 --> 00:48:23.920
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:24.890 --> 00:48:26.300
SPEAKER 2: Thank you, Dr. Arnn.

00:48:26.300 --> 00:48:28.220
We now have time
for a few questions.

00:48:28.220 --> 00:48:29.820
If you would like
to ask a question,

00:48:29.820 --> 00:48:31.883
please come find your
way to a microphone.

00:48:49.160 --> 00:48:51.487
Question to the speaker's left.

00:48:51.487 --> 00:48:53.070
AUDIENCE: Dr. Arnn,
thank you so much.

00:48:53.070 --> 00:48:55.220
Thanks for the invitation.

00:48:55.220 --> 00:48:56.240
We are first-timers.

00:48:56.240 --> 00:48:57.960
We're so glad to be here.

00:48:57.960 --> 00:49:00.740
And I'm so glad you mentioned
Mrs. Thatcher and President

00:49:00.740 --> 00:49:01.820
Reagan.

00:49:01.820 --> 00:49:04.850
Last week, I started to
use one of his quotes

00:49:04.850 --> 00:49:07.760
as my new signature block--

00:49:07.760 --> 00:49:10.520
"The future doesn't belong
to the faint-hearted.

00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:13.438
It belongs to the brave."

00:49:13.438 --> 00:49:14.480
LARRY P. ARNN: That's it.

00:49:14.480 --> 00:49:15.704
Yeah.

00:49:15.704 --> 00:49:17.000
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:24.620
See, the two great
talks we've heard so far

00:49:24.620 --> 00:49:28.610
had many things in common,
and one of the best was,

00:49:28.610 --> 00:49:31.580
speak your piece.

00:49:31.580 --> 00:49:34.470
This not wanting to be rude--

00:49:34.470 --> 00:49:36.620
you shouldn't be rude,
but you shouldn't

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:38.960
be afraid of being called rude.

00:49:38.960 --> 00:49:42.680
And that means, if
somebody is doing something

00:49:42.680 --> 00:49:46.167
that you think is disastrous,
there's only two cures.

00:49:46.167 --> 00:49:47.750
And one of them is
to argue with them,

00:49:47.750 --> 00:49:52.590
and the other is to shoot
them, and arguing with them

00:49:52.590 --> 00:49:54.984
is a lot better.

00:49:54.984 --> 00:49:57.324
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:58.260 --> 00:50:00.710
AUDIENCE: And I'm wearing
Rush Limbaugh's tie.

00:50:00.710 --> 00:50:02.252
LARRY P. ARNN: You're
wearing-- yeah.

00:50:04.500 --> 00:50:07.810
SPEAKER 2: Question to
the speaker's right.

00:50:07.810 --> 00:50:13.010
AUDIENCE: Sir, you've
got a lot to say there,

00:50:13.010 --> 00:50:15.850
but there's one concern I have.

00:50:15.850 --> 00:50:21.490
Right now, if you speak
up, you stand alone.

00:50:21.490 --> 00:50:24.620
And that's a very
dangerous place to be,

00:50:24.620 --> 00:50:28.960
because our government
could just grab you up.

00:50:28.960 --> 00:50:30.830
So what do we do?

00:50:30.830 --> 00:50:34.288
LARRY P. ARNN: Well, first
of all, I envy that beard.

00:50:34.288 --> 00:50:36.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:38.280 --> 00:50:40.592
I bet you get a lot of girls.

00:50:40.592 --> 00:50:42.520
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:43.270 --> 00:50:43.770
Just one.

00:50:43.770 --> 00:50:44.400
You got one.

00:50:44.400 --> 00:50:46.920
Yeah, that's what I got.

00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:48.525
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:49.800 --> 00:50:53.400
It's not the same one,
I don't think, though.

00:50:53.400 --> 00:50:56.460
So the thing is, yeah,
in the beginning.

00:50:56.460 --> 00:51:00.180
But last summer--

00:51:00.180 --> 00:51:03.240
I'm afraid it's going to
be as bad this summer.

00:51:03.240 --> 00:51:05.850
Last summer, all these
events started unfolding,

00:51:05.850 --> 00:51:07.380
and I think that
they're connected.

00:51:07.380 --> 00:51:10.650
And I think that the pandemic
policies, which I think

00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:15.870
are absurd and have
cost many lives--

00:51:15.870 --> 00:51:18.447
whereas if we had identified
and focused on the--

00:51:18.447 --> 00:51:21.030
that's what the Great Barrington
Declaration calls for, right?

00:51:21.030 --> 00:51:23.910
And it just seems
so sensible to me,

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:25.770
especially if you
take into account

00:51:25.770 --> 00:51:28.920
the fact that people shouldn't
have to shut their businesses

00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:30.312
and stay home.

00:51:30.312 --> 00:51:31.770
And you have the
right to assemble.

00:51:31.770 --> 00:51:32.437
What about that?

00:51:32.437 --> 00:51:33.540
Anyway.

00:51:33.540 --> 00:51:37.380
And then that led to,
then, the race riots,

00:51:37.380 --> 00:51:39.210
and then the election.

00:51:39.210 --> 00:51:43.950
And those are violent
things and terrible things

00:51:43.950 --> 00:51:46.260
and the pressures were enormous.

00:51:46.260 --> 00:51:49.020
And so Doug Jeffrey,
who's external affairs

00:51:49.020 --> 00:51:52.620
at the college and my
longest serving colleague--

00:51:52.620 --> 00:51:58.080
pity him-- what did we do on
Imprimis except the pandemic

00:51:58.080 --> 00:52:01.380
and the race riots
and the election?

00:52:01.380 --> 00:52:03.960
And nobody else was doing that.

00:52:03.960 --> 00:52:09.600
And so I thought, OK, we're
going to be destroyed.

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:10.710
We did it anyway.

00:52:14.130 --> 00:52:20.220
I could give you a list
of the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:21.940
Everybody got petitions up--

00:52:21.940 --> 00:52:26.400
social media movements that
you have to make a statement.

00:52:26.400 --> 00:52:28.740
So we had a bunch
of them do that.

00:52:28.740 --> 00:52:30.660
And we don't answer statements.

00:52:30.660 --> 00:52:33.415
If somebody gives me a petition,
before I open it I say,

00:52:33.415 --> 00:52:35.040
well, I'll read this
if you want me to.

00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:37.410
But I promise, whatever
you're asking me to do,

00:52:37.410 --> 00:52:40.310
I'm going to do the opposite.

00:52:40.310 --> 00:52:41.760
You want me to read it?

00:52:41.760 --> 00:52:42.660
Anyway.

00:52:42.660 --> 00:52:44.130
And we don't answer them, right?

00:52:44.130 --> 00:52:49.350
Well, I got ticked off about the
one about Black Lives Matter.

00:52:49.350 --> 00:52:50.940
The thing that got
me was they said

00:52:50.940 --> 00:52:56.520
they were coasting on the
experience of the founders

00:52:56.520 --> 00:53:01.560
of the college, and we've got
to stop talking about them now.

00:53:01.560 --> 00:53:04.590
So we answered that.

00:53:04.590 --> 00:53:08.880
And David Whalen-- he's
a very talented man--

00:53:08.880 --> 00:53:10.500
he wrote most of that.

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:13.005
And it was awesome
and they printed it

00:53:13.005 --> 00:53:14.130
in the Wall Street Journal.

00:53:14.130 --> 00:53:15.960
And then I had
college presidents

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:19.050
from all over the country
write me and praise that.

00:53:19.050 --> 00:53:21.210
And every time I thought,
and where were you?

00:53:24.410 --> 00:53:30.788
So the point is
somebody has to start.

00:53:30.788 --> 00:53:32.756
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:40.650 --> 00:53:42.610
AUDIENCE: I'm a
Hillsdale grandparent,

00:53:42.610 --> 00:53:45.690
so this is my first
time to be here.

00:53:45.690 --> 00:53:50.010
But I was a classroom
teacher for 41 years--

00:53:50.010 --> 00:53:52.950
a teacher, not an educator.

00:53:52.950 --> 00:53:58.020
I really get tired of everybody
being described as educators.

00:53:58.020 --> 00:53:59.840
I feel like I was a teacher.

00:53:59.840 --> 00:54:03.230
And that's all I ever wanted
to do, was teach chemistry.

00:54:03.230 --> 00:54:06.210
I loved my students.

00:54:06.210 --> 00:54:08.400
LARRY P. ARNN: You just
said a lot of information

00:54:08.400 --> 00:54:09.138
about yourself.

00:54:09.138 --> 00:54:10.680
AUDIENCE: Well, I
want to know what's

00:54:10.680 --> 00:54:14.370
your opinion about
educators versus teachers,

00:54:14.370 --> 00:54:16.350
since you describe
yourself as a teacher.

00:54:16.350 --> 00:54:18.090
LARRY P. ARNN:
We're all teachers.

00:54:18.090 --> 00:54:20.527
My dad was a teacher.

00:54:20.527 --> 00:54:22.110
And by the way, you
know what the verb

00:54:22.110 --> 00:54:28.950
that they use for teaching
in the core curriculum--

00:54:28.950 --> 00:54:32.730
in all curriculum talk--

00:54:32.730 --> 00:54:37.290
teaching is called delivery.

00:54:37.290 --> 00:54:41.160
And the point is, teachers
are now like conveyor belts.

00:54:44.670 --> 00:54:46.770
Somebody prepares a package.

00:54:46.770 --> 00:54:48.840
You don't really even
have to know what's in it.

00:54:48.840 --> 00:54:50.970
Just take it down there
and give it to that guy.

00:54:50.970 --> 00:54:52.950
That's the model.

00:54:52.950 --> 00:54:56.430
And it's devastating
and it's demeaning.

00:55:00.290 --> 00:55:05.450
So, in the great Western movies,
there's often a schoolteacher.

00:55:05.450 --> 00:55:09.020
And the cowboys take their hats
off when they go in the school

00:55:09.020 --> 00:55:11.450
and they're polite
around the teacher.

00:55:11.450 --> 00:55:15.140
And the teacher is a symbol
of wisdom in the society,

00:55:15.140 --> 00:55:21.920
and often functions, in the
film, to supply knowledge.

00:55:21.920 --> 00:55:25.850
We always had girls
at Hillsdale College,

00:55:25.850 --> 00:55:29.630
and you had to know Greek and
Latin to get into the college

00:55:29.630 --> 00:55:30.440
back then.

00:55:30.440 --> 00:55:32.130
Our standards have slipped.

00:55:32.130 --> 00:55:33.478
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:33.478 --> 00:55:35.520
And they had a prep school
to teach those things.

00:55:35.520 --> 00:55:37.790
What happened to those women?

00:55:37.790 --> 00:55:41.810
They all became teachers, of
their families and everybody

00:55:41.810 --> 00:55:42.930
in their region.

00:55:42.930 --> 00:55:46.040
And they functioned
as the wisest people

00:55:46.040 --> 00:55:49.580
and most knowledgeable
people on the scene.

00:55:49.580 --> 00:55:52.010
Well, now they're
delivery boys and girls.

00:55:52.010 --> 00:55:55.190
And that's just
devastating demotion.

00:55:55.190 --> 00:55:59.330
And some of them--

00:55:59.330 --> 00:56:03.980
we notice, if you want to get
a job at Hillsdale College,

00:56:03.980 --> 00:56:07.790
do not ever manifest
this point--

00:56:07.790 --> 00:56:10.640
some of them take pride
that they wrote curricula

00:56:10.640 --> 00:56:14.000
that other people had to follow.

00:56:14.000 --> 00:56:19.610
And we write curricula for
15,000 or 20,000 students

00:56:19.610 --> 00:56:22.400
who are studying all the
time, but we write it

00:56:22.400 --> 00:56:24.350
with the teachers.

00:56:24.350 --> 00:56:27.590
So yeah, good point.

00:56:27.590 --> 00:56:30.890
SPEAKER 2: Question to
the speaker's right.

00:56:30.890 --> 00:56:32.450
AUDIENCE: Hi, Dr. Arnn.

00:56:32.450 --> 00:56:36.860
I'm a homeschool mom of
seven, and my first three

00:56:36.860 --> 00:56:39.530
are at Hillsdale.

00:56:39.530 --> 00:56:40.486
LARRY P. ARNN: Mm.

00:56:40.486 --> 00:56:42.876
[APPLAUSE]

00:56:47.280 --> 00:56:49.740
What is your name?

00:56:49.740 --> 00:56:50.955
AUDIENCE: Randi Moody.

00:56:50.955 --> 00:56:52.247
LARRY P. ARNN: Oh, Moody, yeah.

00:56:52.247 --> 00:56:54.936
I'm sorry, I threw
those three out.

00:56:54.936 --> 00:56:57.196
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:57.196 --> 00:57:00.360
AUDIENCE: I understand why.

00:57:00.360 --> 00:57:03.570
I did too.

00:57:03.570 --> 00:57:07.320
But homeschooling is a
countercultural thing to do

00:57:07.320 --> 00:57:09.660
and very difficult.
So my question

00:57:09.660 --> 00:57:12.390
is, what would you
say to encourage

00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:13.562
the homeschool movement?

00:57:13.562 --> 00:57:14.520
LARRY P. ARNN: Oh, man.

00:57:14.520 --> 00:57:16.320
Now, is Dan Peters here--

00:57:16.320 --> 00:57:17.130
the trustee?

00:57:17.130 --> 00:57:19.560
You in the room, Dan?

00:57:19.560 --> 00:57:20.140
Good.

00:57:20.140 --> 00:57:21.906
I was going to praise him.

00:57:21.906 --> 00:57:23.250
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:24.150 --> 00:57:26.100
So I just learned
something exciting.

00:57:26.100 --> 00:57:29.040
First of all, we're going
to do curriculum and video

00:57:29.040 --> 00:57:30.960
and helps for homeschoolers.

00:57:30.960 --> 00:57:32.610
We're starting to
work on that now.

00:57:32.610 --> 00:57:34.998
[APPLAUSE]

00:57:36.970 --> 00:57:39.650
And I'm ignorant.

00:57:39.650 --> 00:57:42.070
And so I never heard of this.

00:57:42.070 --> 00:57:45.580
But in Hillsdale,
Michigan, there's a co-op,

00:57:45.580 --> 00:57:47.200
and that's a bunch
of homeschool moms.

00:57:47.200 --> 00:57:50.110
They're mostly faculty moms, by
the way, that teach part-time

00:57:50.110 --> 00:57:51.940
at the college, some of them.

00:57:51.940 --> 00:57:55.480
And they get together and
they form a little school

00:57:55.480 --> 00:57:58.048
with the kids in their families.

00:57:58.048 --> 00:58:00.340
And now I've just learned
that I have another employee.

00:58:00.340 --> 00:58:04.780
I'm going to hire her husband,
too, I decided the other day.

00:58:04.780 --> 00:58:09.490
And they're in a group of
50 in Jackson, up the road.

00:58:09.490 --> 00:58:11.200
So don't you see?

00:58:11.200 --> 00:58:16.660
I think-- here's
my excited idea--

00:58:16.660 --> 00:58:20.880
that's in fact what
all schools used to be.

00:58:30.190 --> 00:58:34.900
People with the will, who
are reasonably intelligent,

00:58:34.900 --> 00:58:38.170
can build and run
a great school.

00:58:38.170 --> 00:58:41.780
If they get help, it's
better, and we help them.

00:58:41.780 --> 00:58:47.170
But there's no reason,
in K through 12--

00:58:49.675 --> 00:58:54.110
let's say, K through 9--

00:58:54.110 --> 00:58:57.380
there's no real
reason why you need

00:58:57.380 --> 00:58:59.380
an administration in a school.

00:59:03.690 --> 00:59:10.280
And at Hillsdale, we find
that the homeschoolers--

00:59:10.280 --> 00:59:15.440
first of all, it's hard
to get in to Hillsdale,

00:59:15.440 --> 00:59:17.540
so the success
rates are very high.

00:59:20.060 --> 00:59:24.500
Back when there was more
failure than there is now,

00:59:24.500 --> 00:59:28.370
the homeschoolers stood out
because they never failed.

00:59:28.370 --> 00:59:29.270
And why?

00:59:29.270 --> 00:59:33.950
I think one of the challenges
when you get to college

00:59:33.950 --> 00:59:37.400
is there's a lot more
work and it's harder,

00:59:37.400 --> 00:59:40.310
and you've got to
concentrate for longer.

00:59:40.310 --> 00:59:45.100
And homeschoolers are mostly
taught by mom, and she's busy.

00:59:45.100 --> 00:59:46.980
They have to learn
on their own a lot.

00:59:46.980 --> 00:59:48.590
And I think that's
good for them.

00:59:48.590 --> 00:59:52.855
Yeah, and so I encourage
that very much.

00:59:52.855 --> 00:59:55.350
[APPLAUSE]

00:59:58.350 --> 01:00:00.990
SPEAKER 2: Please join
me in thanking Dr. Arnn.

01:00:00.990 --> 01:00:03.740
[APPLAUSE]

